import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parent/'draft-cards.json'
x=json.loads(p.read_text())
def add(cid,body,takeaway,**kw):
 n=len(body.split());assert 30<=n<=50,(cid,n)
 x[cid]=dict(body=body,takeaway=takeaway,**kw)
add('Q1071', 'Geography studies places through the connections between people and environments. Human geography examines societies and economies; physical geography examines landscapes and environmental processes. Bringing them together helps explain why places differ and how human actions contribute to local and global change.', 'Connect social and environmental processes through place.', gap='Broad-field control; no higher field asserted.')
add('local:catalog:equal-earth-projection','Equal Earth is a world-map projection that preserves relative areas while distorting shapes, angles, and distances. This makes it useful for thematic maps comparing area characteristics: a region’s mapped size remains meaningful even when its outline changes.', 'Compare areas without mistaking preserved size for preserved shape.',parent_note='Esri explicitly identifies Equal Earth as a map projection.')
add('local:catalog:natural-earth-projection','Natural Earth is a compromise world-map projection with rounded corners. It preserves neither areas nor angles exactly. High-latitude regions appear enlarged, while angular distortion increases toward the edges, showing why an attractive general-purpose map still requires care when comparing regions.', 'Recognize distortion within a rounded world-map compromise.',parent_note='Esri identifies Natural Earth as a world-map projection.')
add('local:catalog:goode-homolosine-projection','For a spherical Earth, Goode homolosine combines sinusoidal and Mollweide projections to preserve areas. Its familiar interrupted versions split the map into lobes emphasizing land or oceans. These cuts reduce the shape distortion found near the edges of its uninterrupted form.', 'Understand how interruptions trade continuity for reduced shape distortion.',parent_note='Esri identifies Goode homolosine as a map projection.',limits=['Equal-area wording assumes the documented spherical construction; the ArcGIS ellipsoid substitution does not maintain all properties.'])
add('local:catalog:hotine-oblique-mercator-projection','Hotine oblique Mercator aligns a conformal map with a region extending diagonally rather than north–south or east–west. It preserves angles and shapes locally, but area and distance distortion increase away from the central region. Orientation therefore matters when choosing a regional projection.', 'Match a conformal projection to an oblique region.',parent_note='Esri identifies Hotine oblique Mercator as a map projection.')
add('local:catalog:peirce-quincuncial-projection','Peirce quincuncial places the world inside a square that can tile a larger surface. It preserves local angles except at the middle of each edge, where areas become strongly exaggerated. A seamless-looking mosaic therefore does not imply uniformly low distortion.', 'Distinguish map tiling from uniform distortion control.',parent_note='Esri identifies Peirce quincuncial as a map projection.')
add('local:catalog:adams-square-ii-projection','Adams square II maps the world into a square, with the equator and central meridian forming diagonals in its original design. Local angles are preserved except at the corners. Strong area exaggeration near the poles and antimeridian limits what its neat outline conveys.', 'Compare square outlines with local conformality and area distortion.',parent_note='Esri identifies Adams square II as a map projection.')
add('local:catalog:euclidean-buffering','Euclidean buffering draws a distance zone using straight-line measurements on a flat coordinate plane. It works best around features concentrated within a suitably projected small region. Where the projection distorts distance substantially, equal map offsets no longer represent equal distances on Earth.', 'Identify when flat-map distance zones inherit projection distortion.',parent_note='Esri names Euclidean buffering as a geographic buffer construction method.',limits=['Mathematical Euclidean buffering is distinct from ArcGIS’s PLANAR option, which can select geodesic construction for geographic input.'])
add('local:catalog:geodesic-buffering','Geodesic buffering builds distance zones while accounting for Earth’s curved spheroidal surface. It is useful over large extents and near poles or the date line. A zone may look distorted on a flat map while showing the intended distance correctly on a globe.', 'Separate curved-surface distance from flat-map appearance.',parent_note='Esri names geodesic buffering as a geographic buffer construction method.')
add('Q1334127','A sestina reuses six end-words in a prescribed order across six six-line stanzas, then gathers them in a three-line envoi. Its organizing device is recurrence rather than ordinary rhyme. Changing contexts can give the same repeated word a different meaning each time.', 'Hear changing meanings within a fixed end-word cycle.',parent_note='The Academy explicitly classifies sestina as a poetic form.')
add('local:catalog:cento','A cento is a poem assembled entirely from lines by other poets. Its composition depends on selection and juxtaposition: familiar voices meet in unfamiliar combinations, creating irony or humor. The borrowed lines remain recognizable ingredients while their new arrangement creates another poem.', 'Explore how juxtaposition transforms borrowed poetic lines.',parent_note='The Academy explicitly identifies cento as a poetic form.')
add('local:catalog:haibun','Haibun combines prose and haiku, a literary form originating in Japan. The prose often describes an environment and precedes a poem whose connection remains implicit. In Bashō’s travel writing, the surrounding narrative and individual haiku create meaning together without spelling out every link.', 'Read prose and haiku through their implicit connection.',parent_note='The Academy identifies haibun as a literary poetic form.')
add('local:catalog:triolet','A triolet compresses a poem into eight lines and two rhymes. The opening line returns in lines four and seven; the second returns at the end. In Hardy’s “How Great My Grief,” repetition gains emotional weight as the surrounding questions change its force.', 'Notice how a repeated line changes emotional force.',parent_note='The Academy explicitly identifies triolet as a fixed poetic form.')
add('local:catalog:clerihew','A clerihew makes a comic miniature biography from four uneven lines rhyming aabb. The subject’s name appears among the first couplet’s rhyming words. Its deliberately awkward rhythm helps turn a famous person’s life into a playful, deadpan portrait.', 'Use irregular rhythm to make a comic miniature biography.',source_id='culture-aap-clerihew',locator='Definition and From A Poet’s Glossary, lines 28–33',parent_source_id='culture-aap-clerihew',parent_note='The Academy describes clerihew as a comic poetic form.')
add('local:catalog:double-dactyl','A double dactyl is tightly constrained light verse in two four-line stanzas. A nonsense opening and a person’s name fit its recurring beat; shorter closing lines rhyme. Another line must contain one six-syllable word, making vocabulary itself part of the compositional puzzle.', 'Fit comic content to rhythm and a single long word.',source_id='culture-uncg-double-dactyl',locator='Opening instructional paragraphs, lines 0–3',parent_source_id='culture-uncg-double-dactyl',parent_note='The university instructional page identifies this as a verse form.',limits=['The Poetry Archive’s all-lines metrical shorthand is not repeated; UNCG supports the shorter stanza-ending distinction.'])
add('local:catalog:ottava-rima','Ottava rima organizes a stanza into eight lines rhyming abababcc, usually in iambic pentameter. After six lines alternate two rhymes, the closing couplet introduces a paired ending. The form can carry longer poems, including Byron’s Don Juan.', 'Follow alternating rhymes into a closing couplet.',parent_note='The Poetry Archive explicitly identifies ottava rima as a stanza form.')
add('local:catalog:tritina','A tritina contracts the sestina’s pattern of recurring end-words. Three end-words return across three three-line stanzas and meet in a final one-line envoi. Invented by Marie Ponsot, it concentrates formal repetition into a smaller space without abandoning the end-word constraint.', 'Compare a compressed form with its sestina model.',parent_note='The Academy explicitly calls tritina a contraction of sestina.')
add('local:catalog:acrostic','An acrostic hides a word or name in the initial letters of successive poetic lines. Reading down those letters reveals an additional message within the poem. In Blake’s “London,” the third stanza’s initials spell HEAR, reinforcing its attention to troubling sounds.', 'Read a vertical message inside the poem’s ordinary lines.',source_id='culture-aap-acrostic',locator='Definition and Blake example, lines 28–37',parent_source_id='culture-aap-acrostic',parent_note='The Academy explicitly identifies acrostic as a poetic form.')
p.write_text(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print(len(x))
